  "body": "The maintainer asked for this in chat on 2026-11-15.\n\nHis words: \"add a \"theme.json.example\" that has Every damn setting there is for adjusting the look and feel of the UI\"\n\nWhat is missing: the theme files in `assets/themes/` (`horizon-day.json`, `teal-night.json`) show only the settings that each theme sets. A user who writes a theme cannot see the full list of settings.\n\nFor the work:\n\n1. Add `assets/themes/theme.json.example`. It has each setting that a theme file can set: each colour, each font size, each row height, each gap and each radius that `crates/ui/src/egui_app/theme.rs` reads.\n2. Each setting has its default value. Document each setting (what it changes, the unit, the legal range) in a way that the theme loader accepts, or in a table in the user documentation beside the file.\n3. The file loads as a legal theme when the user copies it to a `.json` name. The app does not list the `.example` file as a theme.\n4. Add a test that fails when a theme setting exists in the code and is not in the example file, and when the example file has a key that the loader does not know.\n5. A look-and-feel value that is a fixed number in the UI code and not a theme setting is out of scope here. List each one that you find in a comment on this issue. #2021 moves the ribbon glyph ink and the ribbon row heights into the themes: the example file includes those keys when #2021 merges.\n\nDone means:\n\n- `assets/themes/theme.json.example` has each theme setting with its default value.\n- A test pins that the example file and the loader have the same key set, and that the file loads.\n- The user documentation names the file and says how to make a theme from it.\n",
